<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tipu Sultan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1751">1751</a>–<a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Tiger of Mysore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tipu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> was ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> from 1782, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a>. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he fought the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in three wars, attacked Travancore in 1789 and lost half his kingdom in 1792. He was killed defending his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Seringapatam</a> in 1799, after which the British restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadiyar-dynasty">Wodeyar dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan.jpg" alt="Tipu Sultan" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous artist from Mysore, India. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TipuSultan1790.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
